  🔐 90 Days of Cybersecurity Challenge
Welcome to my 90-Day Cybersecurity Learning Journey! 🚀
I am documenting my day-by-day progress as I develop practical skills in networking, operating systems, ethical hacking, cryptography, web application security, SOC operations, and security governance.
🎯 My Goals
Build strong cybersecurity fundamentals.
Practice hands-on labs using Kali Linux, VirtualBox, Wireshark, Nmap, and other tools.
Understand both offensive (Red Team) and defensive (Blue Team) security.
Share daily notes, screenshots, commands, and lessons learned.
Build a public portfolio and prepare for entry-level cybersecurity roles.
Progress: 0 / 90 days completed. Check each task as you finish it.
Ethics: All scans and security exercises are performed only on systems I own, authorized environments, or training labs.
📚 Daily Roadmap
Week 1: Foundations – How Computers & Networks Talk

Day 1: Learn how the internet works (watch a "how the internet works" primer; understand the basic model).

Day 2: OSI Model — memorize all 7 layers and what happens at each.

Day 3: TCP/IP model — compare it to OSI, understand IP addresses & subnetting basics.

Day 4: Ports & protocols — learn top 20 (80, 443, 22, 21, 25, 53, 3389, etc.) and what they do.

Day 5: DNS — how domain resolution works, record types (A, MX, CNAME, TXT).

Day 6: HTTP/HTTPS — requests, responses, status codes, headers, cookies.

Day 7: Review + quiz yourself on Week 1 (no notes) — redo any weak areas.
Week 2: Networking Deep Dive

Day 8: Routers, switches, firewalls — what each does at a basic level.

Day 9: NAT and private vs. public IP addressing.

Day 10: VPNs — how they work, why they matter for security.

Day 11: Install Wireshark, capture your own traffic, identify a TCP handshake.

Day 12: Use Wireshark to inspect an HTTP request end-to-end.

Day 13: Learn basic subnetting math (practice 10 subnetting problems).

Day 14: Review + rebuild your home network diagram from memory.
Week 3: Operating Systems – Linux Fundamentals

Day 15: Install a Linux VM (Kali or Ubuntu) using VirtualBox/VMware.

Day 16: Learn the Linux filesystem hierarchy (/etc, /var, /home, etc.).

Day 17: Basic commands: ls, cd, pwd, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, cat.

Day 18: File permissions: chmod, chown, understanding rwx.

Day 19: Users & groups: useradd, passwd, sudo, /etc/passwd.

Day 20: Processes & services: ps, top, kill, systemctl.

Day 21: Review — complete an "OverTheWire: Bandit" set of levels (1–10) as practice.
Week 4: Operating Systems – Windows Fundamentals

Day 22: Windows directory structure & registry basics.

Day 23: Windows command line: dir, ipconfig, netstat, tasklist.

Day 24: PowerShell basics: Get-Process, Get-Service, Get-ChildItem.

Day 25: Windows user accounts, groups, and permissions (NTFS).

Day 26: Active Directory — what it is and why it matters (conceptual).

Day 27: Windows Event Viewer — explore logs on your own machine.

Day 28: Review — complete OverTheWire Bandit levels 11–20.
Week 5: Security Fundamentals & Concepts

Day 29: CIA Triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability) — explain it in your own words.

Day 30: Authentication vs. Authorization vs. Accounting (AAA).

Day 31: Threats, vulnerabilities, and risk — how they relate.

Day 32: Common attack types overview (phishing, malware, DoS, MITM — conceptual overview).

Day 33: Defense in depth & the security mindset.

Day 34: Intro to cryptography: symmetric vs asymmetric encryption.

Day 35: Hashing vs encryption vs encoding — know the difference cold.
Week 6: Cryptography & PKI

Day 36: Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) basics — certificates, CAs.

Day 37: TLS/SSL handshake process step-by-step.

Day 38: Hands-on: generate your own SSH key pair, understand public/private key use.

Day 39: Password hashing algorithms (bcrypt, SHA-256) and why salting matters.

Day 40: Digital signatures — how they prove authenticity.

Day 41: Practice: encrypt/decrypt a file using GPG on the command line.

Day 42: Review Weeks 5–6 with flashcards (make 20 cards, quiz yourself).
Week 7: Security Tools & Ecosystem

Day 43: Antivirus/EDR — how modern endpoint detection works (conceptual).

Day 44: Firewalls in depth — stateful vs stateless, ACLs.

Day 45: IDS vs IPS — differences and placement in a network.

Day 46: SIEM basics — what it collects and why (look at Splunk or ELK docs).

Day 47: Set up a free Splunk or ELK instance locally, ingest sample logs.

Day 48: Explore MITRE ATT&CK framework — browse tactics & techniques.

Day 49: Review — map 5 attack types to MITRE ATT&CK tactics from memory.
Week 8: Introduction to Offensive Security

Day 50: Sign up for TryHackMe (free tier) — complete "Pre Security" path intro.

Day 51: Learn the ethical hacking process: recon → scanning → exploitation → reporting.

Day 52: Nmap basics — install it, scan your own home network (with permission).
Day 53: Nmap scan types & flags (-sV, -sC, -A, -p-).

Day 54: Intro to vulnerability scanning concepts (Nessus/OpenVAS overview).

Day 55: TryHackMe: complete a beginner room (e.g., "Nmap" or "Intro to Networking").

Day 56: Review + write a one-page summary of the recon process.
Week 9: Web Application Security Basics

Day 57: OWASP Top 10 — read the list, understand each briefly.

Day 58: SQL Injection — concept, how it works (use PortSwigger Academy).

Day 59: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) — concept and practice on PortSwigger Academy.

Day 60: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) — concept.

Day 61: Broken authentication & session management issues.

Day 62: TryHackMe: complete an OWASP Top 10 room.

Day 63: Review — summarize each OWASP Top 10 item in one sentence, from memory.
Week 10: Defensive Security / Blue Team Basics

Day 64: Log analysis fundamentals — what "normal" vs "suspicious" looks like.

Day 65: Incident response lifecycle (prepare, detect, contain, eradicate, recover, lessons learned).

Day 66: Phishing email analysis — practice identifying red flags in sample emails.

Day 67: Malware basics — types (virus, worm, trojan, ransomware) at a conceptual level.

Day 68: Sandboxing concept — how analysts safely study suspicious files (conceptual overview).

Day 69: TryHackMe: complete a SOC/blue-team-focused beginner room.

Day 70: Review — write a mock incident report for a fictional phishing scenario.
Week 11: Governance, Risk, Compliance & Soft Skills

Day 71: Learn key frameworks: NIST CSF, ISO 27001 (overview level).

Day 72: Common compliance standards: GDPR, HIPAA, PCI-DSS (know what each covers).

Day 73: Risk assessment basics — likelihood × impact.

Day 74: Security policies — what a good policy document includes.

Day 75: Practice explaining a technical concept (e.g., phishing) to a non-technical person.

Day 76: Update your resume/LinkedIn with skills learned so far.

Day 77: Review Week 11 — quiz yourself on framework names & purposes.
Week 12: Certification Prep & Consolidation

DAy 78: Choose your first cert target (CompTIA Security+ recommended) — get the objectives.

Day 79: Take a Security+ practice exam (baseline, no studying) to find weak spots.
Day 80: Study weak spot #1 from practice exam.

Day 81: Study weak spot #2 from practice exam.

Day 82: Study weak spot #3 from practice exam.

Day 83: Take a second practice exam, compare score improvement.

Day 84: Review all flashcards made throughout the 90 days.
Week 13: Portfolio & Next Steps

Day 85: Write up 2–3 TryHackMe rooms you completed as portfolio notes.

Day 86: Set up a GitHub profile, upload your notes/scripts/write-ups.

Day 87: Research entry-level roles (SOC Analyst, IT Support + Security, Jr. Pentester).

Day 88: Join a cybersecurity community (Discord, Reddit r/cybersecurity, local meetup).

Day 89: Plan your next 90 days (pick a specialization: blue team, red team, cloud, GRC).

Day 90: Final review — write a summary of everything you've learned and what surprised you. 


